GPT-6 (Codex; the same model family as round 1).

1. The algorithm-relative control variate uses an inaccurate mean. algrel.py:146–175 computes coarse quadrature; line 265 treats its result as E[eps_hat]. Reproducing the base advice, I obtained 0.128592 with the original grid, 0.130971 with a denser grid, and 0.131694 with another refinement. Independent integration of the identical eps_hat function on 262,144 inputs gave 0.134356 ± 0.000375 SE. Thus the original centering error is about 0.00576, producing bias proportional to beta. results/algrel_T16.json:bar.b therefore confounds description quality with integration error; draft2.md:440–443 cannot attribute its performance solely to weak correlation. The description still substantially overpredicts T16’s true error.

2. Several new sparsity claims fail their stated held-out thresholds, repeating round 1’s problem. results/dag_W64s003ck.json:greedy[tau=0.01].test.flip is 0.0104065, so the reported 57% DAG does not meet 1% (draft2.md:613,626,693); the saved 60% sweep does. results/algrel_W32s001ck.json:dag_rel.targets[tau=0.01].test_flip is 0.0104675, so 54% also fails; the 60% sweep passes. T16’s 1,128-edge algorithm-relative surrogate flips 2.0523%, exceeding its advertised 2% threshold (draft2.md:424). T16’s 58% result does meet 1%. These corrections preserve the qualitative sparsity improvement, with weaker numbers.

3. Equal-FLOP comparisons are numerically wrong. Using mean[p(1−p)] × FLOPs_per_sample/budget from the saved T16 family, the first Neyman and control-variate rows are respectively 8.9× and 6.3× worse than Monte Carlo, not “0.9–1.4x worse to equal” (draft2.md:484; results/bar3_T16.json:b). For M₂,₂, the largest two hybrid budgets beat equal-cost Monte Carlo by approximately 2,100× and 1,200×, not 10⁴–10⁶× (draft2.md:556; results/bar22_ft_s0.001.json:summary). B2 and B3’s numerical thresholds still survive these corrections.

4. Column (a)’s sampling boundary needs to be made explicit. bar3.py:62, bar22.py:22 and algrel.py:255 regenerate each variant’s r through sampled calibration, without charging that work. Under Amendment 5’s literal rule, estimator-side regeneration by sampling belongs in (b). Earlier preregistration explicitly allows recalibration when constructing the model family; if that is the intended boundary, freeze and supply those r values as model parameters, then expose a sampling-free estimator path. The actual edge sets, mean biases and CL advice are correctly base-only (bar3.py:29–38; algrel.py:40–51). Their discovery compute is nevertheless missing beside training compute, as PREREG.md:196–199 requires.

5. Prediction scoring changes the registered experiments. L3 concerns sampling the surrogate directly, including its bias (PREREG.md:166–172,184–185); draft2.md:689 instead scores an unbiased two-level correction implemented in bar3.py:72–77. That experiment cannot settle L3. Amendment 6 specifies W64 at sigma=0.01 (PREREG.md:220), whereas draft2.md:698 substitutes sigma=0.03; that is a separate experiment. Also, lowrank_avg.py:43–49 applies a uniform rank across all steps: its rank 13 does not establish an individual ≥11-direction requirement at every step if L2 is interpreted literally.

6. “No cancellations to exploit” is contradicted by the saved results. For the dp, N_ref=2¹⁴ surrogate, results/ldag_T16_prefix.json gives flip=0.00709534 and dp=0.00108337. These imply 0.3006% of inputs are corrected and 0.4089% are broken: substantial cancellation already occurs. Several stratum loss changes are negative. A positive overall change does not imply every stratum worsens, nor does training establish the claimed optimum (draft2.md:376–380). The supported conclusion is that this search found little additional sparsity under its tolerance constraints.

7. Some training conclusions exceed the checkpoint evidence. draft2.md:19–20 and 593 say from-scratch training fails “at full length,” but the quoted results are explicitly half-length checkpoints. This is a wording/evidence mismatch, not a complaint about the pending final evaluation. Also, eval_ck.py:15 and results/eval_ck524288_*.json:n use 2²⁰ evaluation inputs, whereas draft2.md:129 states 2²² without a checkpoint exception. The saved checkpoint pass/fail labels themselves are consistent with the implemented criterion.

8. Conclusions that survive: the fine-tune table supports T16 at sigma=0.001 as the only passing tested fine-tune; the checkpoint evaluations support wider networks tolerating greater noise. The saved average-targeted DAG counts and per-step flip ranks match the report. The tested ADF closures fail badly; fitted wide-model families predict error more accurately; M₂,₂ retains a large empirical advantage; B2/B3 remain supported at tested budgets. These results establish failures of particular searches and descriptions, not impossibility.

Checked 2026-10-29 03:36 UTC against code/results and read-only CPU checks; no files modified.
